# Generated by Django 5.2.17, then documented and extended with a data backfill.
#
# Decision 25: `Item.track_stock` becomes the single, explicit definition of
# "this item has a stock quantity".
#
# WHY THE BACKFILL IS IN HERE AND NOT OPTIONAL
# -------------------------------------------
# `AddField(default=False)` alone would set every existing PRODUCT to
# untracked, because the flag's default is False. That is silently wrong: those
# items were tracked under the old definition (`item_type == PRODUCT AND
# current_stock IS NOT NULL`), so every existing catalogue would stop moving
# stock the moment 2.1 switches on the ledger, with nothing to tell the user.
#
# The backfill therefore reproduces the OLD queryset exactly:
#     track_stock = (item_type == PRODUCT AND current_stock IS NOT NULL)
#
# It runs BEFORE the constraints, which is required: the constraints forbid
# `track_stock=True` without a non-null quantity, so adding them first would
# reject every row the backfill is about to fix.

